                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 782,
                    "position_y": 26,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 916,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Floors an array from below: every value smaller than the limit is replaced by the limit, and larger values pass untouched.\n\nIt is a clamp on ONE side - the bottom - applied to every element separately, so the output has the same shape as the input. Use it to keep anything downstream from seeing a negative (np.max with max 0 is a rectifier), or to give a level a minimum it never falls below.\n\nTHE NAME READS BACKWARDS:\nnp.max does not find the largest value of the array. It takes, for every element, the larger of that element and the limit (numpy calls this np.maximum) - which keeps the result at or ABOVE the limit, so the limit is a floor. To find the largest value in an array, the reduction, use np.amax.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.max <limit: float>\nDefault 1.0 - so a bare np.max raises everything below 1 up to 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.max -0.5\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe array (a list is turned into one; a single number comes out as a one-value array). Every input is floored and sent - this is the only inlet, and the only thing that makes the node send.\n\nmax (drag float on the node):\nThe floor. A property, not an inlet: it cannot be driven from the patch, and changing it does NOT send by itself - the next array uses it. In the demo make_sine re-sends the wave ten times a second.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout array:\nThe floored array, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nmake_sine sends two cycles of a sine, 128 values from -1 to 1, ten times a second. t array array sends the wave as it is to pack 2 first (its right outlet), then to np.max, whose floored copy completes the pack. The plot draws the two over each other. Above the limit they are one line; below it the wave dips to -1 while the copy runs flat at -0.5. Drag the floor to 0 and every trough is gone. np.amax, under the plot, reads the same wave and sends ONE number, its highest value (1): that is the reduction the name might make you expect.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.min is the ceiling, the other side. np.clip limits both sides at once. np.amax finds the largest value. The max node takes the larger of its input and an operand on an inlet, element by element, so its limit can be driven from the patch. t.maximum is the PyTorch version (the larger of two tensors, element by element).",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 900,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Floors an array from below: every value smaller than the limit is replaced by the limit, and larger values pass untouched.\n\nIt is a clamp on ONE side - the bottom - applied to every element separately, so the output has the same shape as the input. Use it to keep anything downstream from seeing a negative (np.max with max 0 is a rectifier), or to give a level a minimum it never falls below.\n\nTHE NAME READS BACKWARDS:\nnp.max does not find the largest value of the array. It takes, for every element, the larger of that element and the limit (numpy calls this np.maximum) - which keeps the result at or ABOVE the limit, so the limit is a floor. To find the largest value in an array, the reduction, use np.amax.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.max <limit: float>\nDefault 1.0 - so a bare np.max raises everything below 1 up to 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.max -0.5\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe array (a list is turned into one; a single number comes out as a one-value array). Every input is floored and sent - this is the only inlet, and the only thing that makes the node send.\n\nmax (drag float on the node):\nThe floor. A property, not an inlet: it cannot be driven from the patch, and changing it does NOT send by itself - the next array uses it. In the demo make_sine re-sends the wave ten times a second.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout array:\nThe floored array, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nmake_sine sends two cycles of a sine, 128 values from -1 to 1, ten times a second. t array array sends the wave as it is to pack 2 first (its right outlet), then to np.max, whose floored copy completes the pack. The plot draws the two over each other. Above the limit they are one line; below it the wave dips to -1 while the copy runs flat at -0.5. Drag the floor to 0 and every trough is gone. np.amax, under the plot, reads the same wave and sends ONE number, its highest value (1): that is the reduction the name might make you expect.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.min is the ceiling, the other side. np.clip limits both sides at once. np.amax finds the largest value. The max node takes the larger of its input and an operand on an inlet, element by element, so its limit can be driven from the patch. t.maximum is the PyTorch version (the larger of two tensors, element by element)."
                },
